# .env.example
# ===========================================================================
# Jira, Confluence, and GitHub are reached through their MCP servers
# (Atlassian MCP + GitHub MCP), which carry their OWN credentials. With those
# servers configured in your editor/CLI, you should NOT need provider tokens
# here at all — this is the primary, preferred path (see guardrail G5).
#
# This file lists the only environment values the pipeline MIGHT read, for the
# rare case a skill runs in raw-API fallback instead of via an MCP server.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in only what you need. `.env` is git-ignored and is
# never read into agent context or committed. THIS example file is committed,
# so it must contain PLACEHOLDERS ONLY — never a real token, email, or secret.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Jira (fallback only; prefer the Atlassian MCP server) ---
# JIRA_BASE_URL=https://your-jira-host.example.com/
# JIRA_EMAIL=you@example.com
# JIRA_API_TOKEN=<your-jira-api-token>
# JIRA_PROJECT_KEY=EPMCDMETST

# --- Confluence (fallback only; prefer the Atlassian MCP server) ---
# CONFLUENCE_BASE_URL=https://your-site.atlassian.net/
# CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN=<your-confluence-api-token>
# CONFLUENCE_SPACE_KEY=TaskLite

# --- GitHub (fallback only; prefer the GitHub MCP server) ---
# NOTE: .mcp.json reads GITHUB_TOKEN from your SHELL environment (Claude Code
# does not load .env). Set it as a user environment variable, not in this repo.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=<your-github-personal-access-token>
# GITHUB_REPO=https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>
# GITHUB_BASE_BRANCH=main
